  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}=1?$",
  "worked_solution": "The equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $h=-2$ and $k=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(-2,-1).$",
